<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Omani Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1744 CE – 1856 CE · capital Muscat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Al Bu Said maritime empire of Muscat and Zanzibar, which dominated the Swahili coast and the trade of the western Indian Ocean.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 1740s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-said-al-busaidi">Ahmad ibn Said</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sohar">Sohar</a>, expelled the Persian forces that had intervened in the civil war of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yaaruba-dynasty">Ya'aruba dynasty</a> and was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>. His family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-bu-said-dynasty">Al Bu Said</a>, took the title of sayyid and later sultan and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> their capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman's</a> fleets and merchants controlled trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwadar">Gwadar</a> on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/makran-state">Makran</a> coast was acquired in the 1780s and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-abbas">Bandar Abbas</a> leased from Persia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-sultan">Said ibn Sultan</a>, ruling from 1806 to 1856, built up <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Zanzibar</a>, planting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> and promoting the caravan trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves, and moved his residence there around 1840. Treaties were signed with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Said died in 1856 his sons disputed the succession. In 1861 the British-arbitrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canning-award">Canning Award</a> divided the realm into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-muscat-and-oman">Sultanate of Muscat and Oman</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/omani-empire.jpg" alt="Omani Empire" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Muscat.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1744 CE – 1856 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Muscat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ahmad ibn Said, Sultan ibn Ahmad, Said ibn Sultan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ibadi Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Swahili</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/oman">Oman</a>, <a href="/countries/tanzania">Tanzania</a>, <a href="/countries/kenya">Kenya</a>, <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Muscat, Al Said Oman, Busaidi state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=omani-empire" aria-label="Omani Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6242 1307 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Omani Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6562 1563L6532 1571M6562 1563L6086 2664M6562 1563L6562 1563M6562 1563L6335 1476M6562 1563L6101 2585M6562 1563L6101 2585M6562 1563L6140 2512M6562 1563L6136 2518"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6532 1571h0M6086 2664h0M6562 1563h0M6335 1476h0M6101 2585h0M6101 2585h0